            <div class="fs-label">The Method</div>
            <h2>How the Rhythm Library System builds fill and solo vocabulary</h2>
            <p>The Rhythm Library System takes a catalog of sticking patterns and applies them to structured rhythmic sequences. Each pattern creates a different sound and feel when moved around the drums and cymbals. The system works because:</p>
            <div class="fs-why-grid">
                <div class="fs-why-card"><div class="fs-why-icon">🎯</div><h3>Consistent Framework</h3><p>The rhythmic framework stays consistent — you focus on the sticking, not on inventing rhythms on the fly.</p></div>
                <div class="fs-why-card"><div class="fs-why-icon">🔄</div><h3>Small Changes, Big Variety</h3><p>Moving a paradiddle from snare to toms changes the entire character of a fill.</p></div>
                <div class="fs-why-card"><div class="fs-why-icon">📚</div><h3>Organized Vocabulary</h3><p>You build vocabulary systematically rather than randomly — the patterns are catalogued and progressive.</p></div>
                <div class="fs-why-card"><div class="fs-why-icon">⏱️</div><h3>Works at Any Tempo</h3><p>Slow practice builds accuracy. Faster tempos build fluency. The system works at every speed.</p></div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="fs-white">
        <div class="fs-section">
            <div class="fs-label">Fills</div>
            <h2>Applying sticking patterns as drum fills</h2>
            <p>Any sticking pattern becomes a fill when you move it around the kit. Take a simple paradiddle — RLRR LRLL. Play it on the snare and it is an exercise. Assign the right hand to a tom and the left to the snare, or alternate toms on each double stroke, and it becomes a fill with shape and movement.</p>
            <p>The Rhythm Library System gives you dozens of patterns to work with. Each one has a different accent structure and feel. Practicing them through the rhythm sequences builds the muscle memory so the patterns are available when you need them in a musical context.</p>
            <blockquote class="fs-quote">"The same pattern sounds completely different depending on where you place it on the kit. That is the key insight — sticking patterns are not snare drum exercises. They are musical phrases waiting to be orchestrated."</blockquote>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="fs-gray">
        <div class="fs-section">
            <div class="fs-label">Solos</div>
            <h2>Building drum solos from pattern vocabulary</h2>
            <p>A solo is a conversation — call and response, tension and release, repetition and variation. When your sticking pattern vocabulary is deep, you can:</p>
            <ul class="fs-list">
                <li>State a pattern as a motif, then develop it across the kit</li>
                <li>Shift accent placement to create dynamic contrast</li>
                <li>Connect patterns smoothly because you know how each one starts and ends</li>
                <li>Return to a theme after a contrasting section for musical structure</li>
            </ul>
            <p>Gordy Knudtson designed the Rhythm Library System from decades of professional performing experience. The system reflects how working drummers actually think about fills and solos — not as tricks, but as vocabulary drawn from organized practice.</p>
        </div>
    </div>

